From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] QThreadData: forget the cached QBindingStatus when a thread drops its data

deref_current_thread_data() resets currentThreadData but leaves the
thread_local tl_status pointing at the QBindingStatus owned by the
QThreadData it just released.

If anything on the same native thread runs Qt code after that, for
example a C++ thread_local destructor that uses QThreadStorage (KF6
keeps KSharedConfig in thread_locals, e.g. KIconLoader::global() and
KColorScheme's defaultConfig(), and ~KSharedConfig calls
QThreadStorage::hasLocalData()), QThreadData::current() adopts the
thread again and the new QAdoptedThread takes the stale status through
getBindingStatus().
Both QThreadData objects then own the same QBindingStatus and the
second ~QThreadData frees it again.

This happens wherever C++ thread_local destructors run after pthread
key destructors, which is the case with libstdc++'s
__cxa_thread_atexit fallback on musl. On glibc the order is the other
way round, so the bug stays latent there, but the fix is plain C++
and correct on any libc.

Seen as Elisa crashing in ~QThreadData (free of the QBindingStatus)
when its QQuickPixmapReader thread exits, either in that thread or in
the main thread when it deletes the QThread.
---
diff -ruN a/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty.cpp b/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty.cpp
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty.cpp
@@ -2522,6 +2522,17 @@
     tl_status = status;
 }
 
+/*!
+    \internal
+    Forgets the binding status cached for the current thread. Called when the
+    thread's QThreadData (which owns the QBindingStatus) is released, so that
+    a later re-adoption of the same native thread allocates a new one.
+ */
+void clearBindingStatus(QBindingStatusAccessToken) noexcept
+{
+    tl_status = nullptr;
+}
+
 namespace PropertyAdaptorSlotObjectHelpers {
 void getter(const QUntypedPropertyData *d, void *value)
 {
diff -ruN a/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty_p.h b/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty_p.h
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty_p.h
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qproperty_p.h
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 namespace QtPrivate {
     Q_CORE_EXPORT bool isAnyBindingEvaluating();
     struct QBindingStatusAccessToken {};
+    void clearBindingStatus(QBindingStatusAccessToken) noexcept;
 
     namespace BindableWarnings {
     Q_CORE_EXPORT void printSignalArgumentsWithCustomGetter();
diff -ruN a/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp b/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
--- a/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp
@@ -187,6 +187,16 @@
     // ... but we must reset it to zero before returning so we aren't
     // leaving a dangling pointer.
     currentThreadData = nullptr;
+
+    // The same goes for the cached QBindingStatus: it is owned by the
+    // QThreadData we just released, which may already be gone (or will be
+    // once the QThread is destroyed). If anything running later on this
+    // native thread (e.g. a C++ thread_local destructor that uses
+    // QThreadStorage, which on some C libraries runs after this pthread key
+    // destructor) adopts the thread again, the new QAdoptedThread must get a
+    // fresh QBindingStatus instead of taking ownership of this one, or both
+    // QThreadData objects end up deleting the same QBindingStatus.
+    QtPrivate::clearBindingStatus({});
 }
 
 static void destroy_auxiliary_thread_data(void *p)
